{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 17, :comments ["<b>Speak to Aaron, saying.</b> It says ״saying,״ because the commandment came to Aaron as He said ״Speak to Aaron״ — He placed upon him the obligation to separate those with blemishes from the priests, since he is the High Priest. Therefore it says <b>saying</b> — that he should also warn all the priests, by stating the commandment to them. Thus the priests are warned on their own account, and the High Priest is likewise obligated to ensure that no man with a blemish approaches." "<b>Any man of your seed, etc.</b> It did not say it in the same order as it said at the beginning of the parashah, nor in the same order as it said in the parashah after it: ״Speak to Aaron and to his sons and they shall separate themselves,״ nor in the order of wine and strong drink where it said ״you and your sons״ (Leviticus 10:9). And perhaps HaShem intended, with the sweetness of His expression, to say that Aaron and his sons are secure from all the blemishes mentioned in the parashah; therefore He mentioned neither him nor his sons, but rather ״your seed.״ And even though his sons too are called ״your seed,״ nevertheless, from the fact that He changed the wording, He has alerted you that it is concerning those who come after them that He speaks. And He was precise, too, in saying ״of your seed״ — He assured you that the blemish would not fall upon all of them but only upon some of them. And see the commentary on the verse ״והוא צרוע״ (Leviticus 22:4)."], :book :leviticus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 21, :notes {0 "The text has parenthetical variant readings: יִגַּשׁ ('approach') and יָזִיד, which I have translated according to context as the verb governing the clause 'that no man with a blemish should approach.'"}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T20:00:09.045598530Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}